From e2d6b8d891b27affcddcabbe85661bda8a4ad90d Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Ian Blenke <ian@blenke.com>
Date: Mon, 6 Jul 2026 19:56:10 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 05936/11362] [outer-loop] pursue the exp4544 Family-B
 counterexample gap: pass real mismatch evidence to refactor()

Operator asked to pursue the two previously-identified-but-unbuilt
ARC-AGI-3 alternatives. Investigated exp4544's GOAL+DYNAMICS proposer
first (docs/research-notes/arc-llm-inducer-sota-420.md's .421 candidate,
still unbuilt_mapped_only as of 2026-07-04 per
docs/research-notes/arc-agi3-levers-tried-x-verdict-2026-06-25.md).

Found the actual code (python/carnot/agentic/arc_executable_world_model.py
+ arc_llm_reinduction.py) is more sophisticated than the SOTA note
assumed -- it already does Family-B-style executable induction with real
held-out verification (WorldModelVerifier, min_heldout_accuracy=1.0 on
the live path) and bounded refinement (<=3 rounds), and is already
live-path-reachable from E3AgentPolicy with no wiring needed.

The real, narrower gap: on the dominant live failure mode
(heldout_transition_verification_failed), execute_bounded_llm_reinduction
built a FAKE counterexample -- a scalar summary dict
({"kind", "heldout_accuracy"}) wrapped by _counterexample_result() into a
SimpleNamespace with hardcoded n=1/n_correct=0/accuracy=0.0 -- even
though WorldModelVerifier.score() (already imported elsewhere in this
same codebase) computes REAL per-transition mismatches (BEFORE/PREDICTED/
OBSERVED deltas) that refactor_prompt() is already built to consume
(vr.mismatches[:5], formatted for the LLM). The LLM refactor step was
getting "you're wrong" with zero concrete detail to fix, exactly the
counterexample-guided-refinement gap arXiv:2606.11521 (cited by the
original SOTA note) describes.

Fix: when a candidate is rejected, call
WorldModelVerifier(transitions).score(selected.engine) to get real
n/n_correct/accuracy/mismatches, attach them to last_counterexample as
real_n/real_n_correct/real_accuracy/real_mismatches, and have
_counterexample_result() use them when present (falling back to the old
scalar-only behavior when absent, for safety). Confirmed via the
existing test's own failure: before the fix, refactor() got
n=1/accuracy=0.0 always; after, it gets the genuine n=2/n_correct=1/
accuracy=0.5 with the actual failing transition's delta.

Updated tests/python/test_experiment_4557_executable_world_model_proposer.py's
existing test (which had hardcoded mismatches[0]['kind'], effectively
locking in the bug) to assert the real evidence flows through instead --
this is more faithful to the test's own stated intent ("held-out
transition failures become CEGIS counterexamples") than what it
previously verified. Ran the full directly-relevant test suite (51
tests across 8 files touching arc_llm_reinduction/arc_world_model_trust_
energy/arc_executable_world_model) clean. Two pre-existing, unrelated
failures in test_arc_world_model_trust_energy.py and
test_experiment_4801 confirmed via git stash to fail identically on
unmodified code -- not caused by this change.

This is a targeted enrichment, not the full exp4544 gap (GOAL and
DYNAMICS are still proposed/verified as one combined candidate, not
independently -- a separate, larger architectural change, not attempted
here). The TRM-as-sequence-refiner alternative was investigated but not
pursued further this session, per the honest conclusion in
docs/research-notes/trm-leave-one-game-out-pilot-results-2026-07-05.md.

